## High-Purity Inorganic Perovskite Films for 9.72%-Efficiency Solar Cells
Solution-processed organic-inorganic lead halide hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are attracting tremendous interests owing to their high power conversion efficiency (PCE) over 22 per cent.[1-7] The major challenges for large-scale commercialization of hybrid PSCs include performance attenuation upon humidity and thermal attacks as well as iodide ion diffusion from lead halides to metallic electrodes,[8,9] and high cost of state-of-the-art hole-transporting materials.[10,11] Perovskite-structured cesium lead bromide \((\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3})\) having high carrier mobility and unchanged crystal structure is relatively stable in humid and thermal atmospheres, therefore it is regarded as a promising light-harvesting absorber for all-inorganic PSC platforms.[12-14]
However, the PCE enhancement of all-organic \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) PSCs is significantly limited by developed solution-processed techniques due to large concentration differences between CsBr and \(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\) solutions (Figure S1). Therefore, mixed phases are always formed in corresponding perovskite films. X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern confirms the presence of CsBr-rich \(\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) phase in this inorganic lead halide film, reducing the total PCE level of solar cells (Figure S2). To address this profound issue, we launch here a multi-step solution-processed method to optimize material components, crystal structure and morphological alignment from monolayer \(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\) and multiple CsBr. As shown in Figure 1a,b, compact \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) \((\mathsf{c}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2})\) underlayer is spin coated on a transparent \(\mathsf{F T O}\) \((\mathsf{F}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2})\) glass subsequently, 200-nm-thick mesoscopic \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) \((m\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2})\) is made by coating colloidal \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) particles onto \(\mathsf{c}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) underlayer and calcining at \(450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air. 1 M \(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\) N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solution is then spin-coated onto \(m\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) layer to form a solid \(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\) layer and 0.07 M CsBr in ethanol is further spin-coated for one to six cycles. Finally, the solar cell is covered by a carbon back-electrode. Our results show that the crystal phase of inorganic halides converts from \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) \((n\leq3)\) to \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) \((n=4)\) and subsequent to \(\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) \((n\geq5)\). Upon interfacial decoration by graphene quantum dots (GQDs) for enhanced charge extraction and reduced charge recombination, the final solar cell achieves a maximized PCE of \(9.72\%\). When persistently attacked by \(90\%\) humidity at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) or \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at zero humidity, the optimal device remains \(87\%\) over 130 days or \(95\%\) over 40 days of initial efficiency, respectively. The launched multi-step solution-processed method provides new opportunities for all-inorganic PSCs with unprecedented efficiency, high stability and cost-effectiveness.
