Figure 1c presents the top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of cesium lead bromide films on \(\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{c}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{m}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\). The film coverage and average grain
size are low at \( n\leq2 \), and pinholes are found at \( n=3 \). By increasing deposition cycle to \( n=4 \), both film coverage and grain size are ideal. Further deposition of CsBr solution reduces grain size and therefore increases charge-transfer paths and charge recombination at grain boundaries. Deep observation demonstrates that the average grain size is \( 300\;\mathsf{n m} \) at \( n=1 \) and increases to maximized \( 1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}} \) at \( n=4 \) and further declines to
\( 400\;\mathsf{n m} \) at \( n=6 \). The cross-sectional SEM photographs in Figure 1d reveals a compact cesium lead bromide film with vertical and monolayer-aligned largest grains is formed, which is expected to maximize charge transportation kinetics.
By spin-coating CsBr solution onto \( \mathsf{P b B r}_{2} \) layer, the film color immediately changes from white to yellow, referring to the formation of cesium lead bromide halide. XRD profiles are used
to study the formation mechanism of perovskite film, as shown in Figure 2a,b and Figure S3, indicating a phase conversion of \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\;\;\to\;\;\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\;\;\to\;\;\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) during the multi-step processes.[15-17] In details, the crystalline conversion can be summarized into three reactions as follows:
\(2\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}+\mathsf{C s B r}\to\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) (n≤3)
\[
\mathsf{CsPb}_2\mathsf{Br}_5+\mathsf{CsBr}\to2\mathsf{CsPbBr}_3(n=4)
\]
\[
\mathsf{CsPbBr}_3+\mathsf{CsBr}\to\mathsf{Cs}_4\mathsf{PbBr}_6(n\geq4)
\]
In details, \(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\) \((2\theta=18.6^{\circ}\) , Figure 2b) is rich at \(n\leq3\) therefore reaction (1) is dominated to form \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) phase \((2\theta\) \(=11.7^{\circ}\) , 29.4°). Subsequently, \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) reacts with CsBr at \(n=\) 4 to form perovskite \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) \((2\theta=15.3^{\circ}\) , \(21.7^{\circ})\) , as shown in reaction (2). Further increase in CsBr cycle leads to conversion of \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) to CsBr-riched \(\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) phase \((2\theta=12.7^{\circ}\) , \(12.9^{\circ}\) \(27.6^{\circ}\) , \(28.7^{\circ})\) according to reaction (3). This deduction can be cross-checked by the peak intensity evolution of \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) and \(\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) (Figure 2a,b) and atomic \(\mathsf{C s}/\mathsf{P b}\) ratio from EDS spectra (Figure 2c and Figure S4). The phase separation of \(\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) from \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) may also split large grains into smaller ones. In this fashion, the large \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) grains and vertical crystallinity arise from the recrystallization and recoalescence of smaller crystalline grains along with the expand of grain volume \((\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\ <\ \mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\ <\ \mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6})\) , which is accordance with the thickness of perovskite films in their crosssectional SEM images and previous report.[18] Actually, the multistep technique resembles phase fusion and separation along with spin-coating CsBr solution: \(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\to\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\to\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) \(\rightarrow\mathsf{C s}_{4}\mathsf{P b B r}_{6}\) . A noticeable structural transformation can be observed in the depiction of crystallographic structures (Figure 2d), which can also be cross-checked by the calculated XRD profiles from three schematic diagrams (Figure S5). It should be noted that the \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) can not be consumed thoroughly during the whole spin-coating processes, which is originated from the transition of \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) to tetragonal \(\mathsf{C s P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{5}\) crystals under elevated temperature.[15]
HTL-free all-inorganic PSC device with \(\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{c}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{m}\mathrm{-}\) \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}/\) carbon architecture is made using the multi-step solution-processed technique in air atmosphere, as shown in Figure 2e. The device has typical layered structure, having average thicknesses for \(m{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) , vertical \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) and carbon electrode of \(200\,\mathrm{\nm}\) , \(400\,\mathrm{\nm}\) and \(15\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) , respectively. The current density (J)-voltage \((V)\) characteristics of the PSC devices are recorded under simulated air mass 1.5 global (AM1.5G) solar illumination to compare their photovoltaic performances. Figure 2f shows the \(J_{-}V\) curves measured at a light intensity of \(100\ m W\ c m^{-2}\) and the photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 1. The short-circuit current density \((J_{\infty})\) , open-circuit voltage \((V_{\infty})\) , fill factor \((F F)\) and PCE all increase by tuning CsBr deposition cycle from one to four and subsequently reduce beyond four cycles, yielding maximized \(J_{\infty},V_{\infty},F F\) , and PCE of \(7.46\;\;\mathsf{m A}\;\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) , 1.308 V, \(77.3\%\) and \(7.54\%\) for the device deposited by four-cycle CsBr, respectively.
In the current work, the PCE optimization mainly arises from the increased crystallinity and prolonged carrier lifetime of perovskite-structured \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) according to photo-luminescence (PL) and time-resolved PL decay characterizations, as shown in Figure S6. This conclusion agrees with SEM observation, XRD profiles and UV-vis absorption spectra (Figure S7). In comparison with state-of-the-art organic-inorganic PSCs, the lower PCE of all-inorganic PSCs is attributed to a \(2.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) -wide bandgap of \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) (Figure S7), which is much higher than \({\sim}1.5\) eV for \(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}\) and \({\sim}1.4\) eV for \(\mathsf{C H}(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}\) halides.[19] The IPCE spectra in Figure \(\S2g\) demonstrate these all
inorganic PSCs have photoelectric conversion in wavelength range of \(325{\sim}550\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the maximal IPCE value is around \(93\%\) for the device made at \(n=4\) . In addition, the space-charge limited current (SCLC) tests also demonstrate an increased crystallinity of cesium lead bromide quasi-perovskite films and therefore electron mobility (Figure S8).[20,21]
The large energy barrier at \(m{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}\) \((E_{\mathrm{CB}}=-4.0\ \mathrm{eV})/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) \((E_{\mathrm{CB}}=-3.5\ \mathrm{eV})\) interface significantly limits electron extraction and therefore PCE enhancement.[22] To address this issue, single- or few-layer GQDs with average diameter of \({\sim}5~\mathsf{n m}\) are homogeneously distributed on \(m{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}\) film (Figure S9,S10). By setting an integrated energy level of \(-3.84~\mathrm{eV}\) at interface, as shown in Figure 3a and Figure S11, the solar cell yields an unprecedented PCE as high as \(9.72\%\) with \(J_{\infty}\) of \(8.12\ m{\sf A}\ c m^{-2}\) \(V_{\infty}\) of \(1.458~\mathrm{V}\) and \(F F\) of \(82.1\%\) and good reproducibility (Figure S12). This efficiency enhancement arises from reduced interfacial energy barrier and increased electron extraction. Steady PL spectra are measured for \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) and \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{G Q D s}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) films to reveal the potential mechanism of increased electricity output. As shown in Figure S13, under the same experimental condition, the emission peak intensity centered at \(530\ \mathsf{n m}\) of \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{G Q D s}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) film is obviously quenched nearly \(90\%\) with respect to the \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) film, arising from the effective carrier extraction ability of GQDs from \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) .[23] Figure 3b presents the time-resolved PL decay spectra of the films with and without GQDs. A shorter electron decay lifetime (99.3 ns) for \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{G Q D s}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) compared with 116.2 ns for \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) confirms the high electron extraction from \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) halide.
