Figure 4a and Figure S14 present the normalized PCE, \(J_{\mathsf{S c}}\), \(V_{\infty}\) and \(F F\) of two photovoltaics upon attack by \(90\%\) RH at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The GQDs tailored PSC remains \(87\%\) of initial efficiency over 130 days, which is much higher than \(79\%\) for pristine device. A possible mechanism behind enhanced stability by setting GQDs is the restriction effect of ultrathin GQDs for ultraviolet light illumination to degrade perovskite \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) film (Figure S15).[28] The high-temperature operation at \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) may decompose perovskite-structured \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) film. Upon persistently thermal attack at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and zero humidity, as shown in Figure 4b, all the normalized PCEs are almost unchanged over 40 days because of relatively high decomposition temperature of \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) beyond \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).[29,30]
In summary, the multi-step solution-processed technique provides a new way of making high-performance, HTL-free, all-inorganic PSCs. Phase conversion is studied for high-purity \(\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) film. An unprecedented PCE of \(9.72\%\) is achieved by modifying \(m\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}/\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}\) interface with GQDs. Upon persistently attacks by high humidity and temperature, the photovoltaic device made in optimized conditions is still relatively stable over more than 130 days. The advantages of high-efficiency, improved stability and cost-effectiveness forward the commercialization of PSC platforms.
